&= 4 \left(\sin{2\theta} \right)^2 = 4\left[ 1 - \cos^2{2\theta} \right] = 4 - 4\cos^2{2\theta}\\
\therefore \cos^2{2\theta} &= 1 - 8 \sin^4{\theta}, \text{ which gives us a fourth-degree equation for the angle } \theta.
\end{align}

Now we have an equation for finding $\theta.$ Since we have two possibilities for the position of the center of the third circle, we can calculate both solutions. The specific numerical solution for $\theta$ depends on the value of $\sin{\theta}$, which unfortunately requires finding the exact square root of a fourth-degree polynomial. Without a numerical approach, we are unable to obtain a simplified exact solution.

