First note that the least-common multiple of $8m$ and $10^{10}$ is the smallest number divisible by both of them, so  $4\cdot\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]$ has at least an $8$ and a $10^{10}$, so it must be greater or equal to $8\cdot 10^{10}$

Since  $4\cdot\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]$ divides $\mathop{\text{lcm}}[8m,10^{10}]$, let $k=\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]$. Then:

\begin{align*}
\mathop{\text{lcm}}[8m,10^{10}] & = 4\cdot k\\
\mathop{\text{lcm}}[8,10^{10}] & = 80\cdot 10^{10}\\
\implies k & \leq \frac{80\cdot 10^{10}}{8} =10^{10}
\end{align*}

Thus, for $k$ to be the least possible, we need $k =10^{10}$, and this leads to:

\begin{align*}
\mathop{\text{lcm}}[8m,10^{10}] & = 4\cdot k = 4\cdot 10^{10}\\
\implies  8m & = 4\cdot 10^{10} \tag*{(since $\gcd[8,10]=4$)}
\end{align*}

Let $abcd \in \{100, 101, \dots, 999\}$ represent $m$, i.e., $m = 10^3 \cdot a + 10^2 \cdot b + 10^1 \cdot c + d$, so:

\begin{align*}
\implies 8 \cdot 10^3 \cdot a + 8 \cdot 10^2 \cdot b +8 \cdot 10^1 \cdot c + 8d & = 4 \cdot 10^{10} \\
\end{align*}

We have:

\begin{align*}
(8d - 4)(10^3 \cdot a + 10^2 \cdot b +10^1 \cdot c) & = 4 \cdot 10^7 \cdot 10^3 \\
\end{align*}

Thus, since the only possible larger common factor of  $10^3, 10^2$ and $10^1$ is the number of digits in $m$ (which is $3$), one can infer that  $d \ne 0$, otherwise the equation above would contradict the fact that  $k=\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}] =10^3\cdot a + 10^2 \cdot b +10^1 \cdot c =10^3$ (assuming $d=0$), which isn't possible.

Thus, we must have  $8d - 4=\pm 10^4 \cdot t$, and since $d \ne 0$, we must have  $8d-4=10^4 \cdot t$, and logically, $t>0$ (otherwise we'd be having a larger common factor among \set{1000, 100, 10} than one digit, which is $10$). Then,

\begin{align*}
