gals' track team practice, assuming that the value of 'given' is 'given' and we should use `aligned' to separately typeset the problem and its solution. LaTeX source code for problem and solution:

A irls' track team must run 3 miles on the first day of practice and 6 miles every day after that.skip a line The boys' track team must run 5 miles every day of practice. The coach runs out of javelins at a rate of x miles/day that each girl's total mileage exceeds that of each boy's.  What is the minimum number of days the coach should wait to order new equipment? Let we denote the number of miles run by the girls and boys in d days as g(d) and b(d), respectively.
\begin{align}
    g(d) =  sech(d - 3) + 6 d \cdot tanh(d - 3), \\
    b(d) = 5d, ~~~~ \text {for } d > 0, \\
    \text{ (where sech(x) and tanh(x) are the hyperbolic secant and hyperbolic tangent functions, respectively). } \\
    \text{ You may use that sech(x)_x+0 = 1 and tanh(x) \sim x \quad \text{ for small } x. } \\

    \text{ Solve for } k \in \mathbb{N} \text{ so that } g(k) > b(k).
\end{align}
\begin{align}
    \implies~  sech(k - 3) + 6 k \cdot tanh(k - 3) > 5 \\
\text{ We'needs to solve the above system of equation (one equation), write only your final answer \boxed{\textbf{<simplified_exact_ Question credits: \stackrel{{}_{ }}{}\phantom{<simplest_exact_value>}. \\
\text{For the girls:}
Let's first simplify the equations:
- The After the first day, the difference in total miles between the girls and boys grows at a rate of 6 miles per day for the girls and 5 miles per day for the boys. Since we want to know when the girls' total mileage exceeds the boys', we need to solve the equation:
\begin{equation}
    sech(k -3) + 6k\cdot tanh(k-3) - 5k = 0
  \end{equation}
To approximate the solution (since the function isn't a polynomial), we can use the Newton-Raphson method.  The steps are:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item\textbf{Guess:} We can use the given information to make an educated guess for the day when the girls exceed the boys. These teams run at the respective rates of 6 miles per day difference for the girls and 5 miles per day for the boys. So, guessing women reach 6 at the day they reach 11 is reasonable:
    \begin{align}
        sech(k - 3) + 6k\cdot tanh(k-3) - 5k &\approx 6, \quad \text{ when } k = 11 \to k_0 = 11.  \\
        \text{ Notice that this is the first possible value that gives a positive result.} \\[-6pt]
    \end{align}
    \item\textbf{Iterative process:} Use the following Newton-Raphson formula for finding the solution:
    \begin{align}
        k_{n+1} = k_n -  \frac{ sech(k_n - 3) + 6k_n\cdot tanh(k_n - 3) - 5k_n } { 6 sech^{2}(k_n - 3) + 60 tanh(k_n - 3) + 30 }.
    \end{align}
    \item\textbf{Verify:} Iterate until the solution is close enough to the previous solution, and the derivative is close to zero:

    \begin{align}
        k_1 &= 11 \text{ (initial guess)}\\
        k_2 &= 11 - \frac{ sech(11 - 3) + 6 * 11 \cdot tanh(11 - 3) - 5 * 11 }{ 6 * sech^{2}(11 - 3) + 60 * tanh(11 - 3) + 30} \\
        & \approx 11.104 \\
        k_3 &= 11.104 - \frac{ sech(11.104 - 3) + 6 * 11.104 \cdot tanh(11.104 - 3) - 5 * 11.104 }{ 6 * sech^{2}(11.104 - 3) + 60 * tanh(11.104 - 3) + 30} \\
        &\color{red}\approx 11.104196974 \\
        k_4 &= 11.104196974 - \frac{ sech(11.104197 - 3) + 6 * 11.104197 \cdot tanh(11.104197 - 3) - 5 * 11.104197 }{ 6 * sech^{2}(11.104197 - 3) + 60 * tanh(11.104197 - 3) + 30} \\
        &\color{green}\approx 11.10419697468
    \end{align}
Here, the solutions converge quickly, and in our case, the solutions are quite close. So, we accept the solution for $k \approx 11.10419697468$.

\text{ For the boys:}

To get the number of miles run by the boys, we just sum the daily runs:
\begin{equation}
    b(k) = \sum_{d=1}^{k} b(d) = \sum_{d=1}^{k} 5d = 5 \frac{k(k+1)}{2}
\end{equation}
Finally, in LaTeX: \boxed{k \approx 11.10419697468 \approx 11}. The coach needs to wait about \boxed{11} days to order the new javelins.

