Regarding your question about the remainder when a polynomial is divided by a quadratic, this computation uses synthetic division or polynomial long division, which work for any polynomial and quadratic divisor. When you divide a polynomial $p(x)$ by a quadratic $q(x) = a(x-r)^2 + s$, the remainder $r(x)$ is always a polynomial that has degree less than the degree of the divisor. In this case, we find that the remainder $r(x)$ has a degree of 1 (i.e., linear), which corresponds to the fact that the divisor $q(x)$ has a degree of 2. In general, when you divide a polynomial of degree $n$ by a quadratic, the remainder will be at most a polynomial of degree $n-2$.

