First, let's find the volume of the 2-cm-by-2cm-by-2cm cube:
\boxed{8 \text{ cubic centimeters}}

Next, we know that the volume of the larger cube is 20 cubic centimeters, which is 19 cubic centimeters greater than the original volume:

\boxed{20 - 8 = 12 \text{ cubic centimeters}}

Since the larger cube is also a cube, its length, width, and height must all be equal to the edge length of the larger cube. Let this edge length be L (cm). The formula for the volume of a cube is:

Volume = side length³

Setting this equal to the given volume:

\boxed{ L³ = 12}

To solve for L, we take the cube root of both sides:

\boxed{ L = \sqrt[3]{12}}

To get the simplest exact value, we rewrite the cubed root of 12 in whole number form:

\boxed{ L = 2 \sqrt[3]{3}}

\boxed{ 2 \sqrt[3]{3}} \text{ cubic centimeters}

completions_4:
Solution:

1. First, let's find the volume of the original cube. Since it is a cube, volume equals every edge cubed. So, the volume of the original 2-cm cube is $\boxed{ 8 \text{ cubic cm } }$.

2. Since the given problem states that the volume increased by 19 cubic cm, the volume of the new cube is the sum of the original volume and the increase, resulting in $\boxed{27 \text{ cubic cm }}$.

3. Again, the volume of a cube is every edge cubed, so we can now set up an equation:
   \begin{equation}
    (x)^3 = 27
   \end{equation}
   where x is the new length of one edge of the larger cube.

4. Cube roots are exact, so finding the cube root in computers or calculators may give an approximate value (with decimal places). However, in this case:
   \begin{equation}
     x = \sqrt[3]{27}
   \end{equation}
   gives us the exact value of x as 3.

5. Adding units, the final answer is $\boxed{3 \text{ cm }}$.

6. I encourage anyone who enjoyed this problem and wants more of a similar style to visit <https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Problem_Solving_Basics> for further challenges. Happy problem-solving!

completions_5:
Here we go:

1. Calculate the volume of the original cube: \begin{aligned}
	V &= (base \times height \times depth) && \text{(Formula for the volume of a cube)}\\
	V &= 2\, \text{cm} \times 2\, \text{cm} \times 2\, \text{cm}
\end{aligned}

2. Calculate the new volume of the increased cube:

\begin{aligned}
	V &= \boxed{\color{blue} {2^3}} \times \text{units} \\
	&= 8\, \text{ cubic cm} \times \text{units}
\end{aligned}

The original volume plus the 19 cubic centimeters is the new volume:

\begin{aligned}
	(\boxed{2^3} + 19) \times \text{units} &= 8\, \text{ cubic cm} \times \text{units}\\
	6.01 \times \text{units} &= 8\, \text{ cubic cm} \times \text{units}\\
	\text{units} &= \boxed{\frac{8 \times \text{units}}{6.01}} = \boxed{\frac 8 6\, \text{ units}}
\end{aligned}
After this, substituting the length of one edge in the Volume Formula and defining the new edge length as `e`,

\begin{aligned}
	\boxed{(e)^3} &= (2\, \text{cm})^3 + 19 \times (2\, \text{cm})^3 && \text{(Formula for the volume of a cube)}\\
	8\, \text{ cubic cm} + 19 \times 8 \, \text{ cubic cm} &= (e)^3\\
	\boxed{272 } &= (e)^3 && \text{(Because the total volume increased by } 19 \times \boxed{2^3} \text{ was 272 cubic centimeters)}
\end{aligned}

By taking the cube root of both sides:

\boxed{e} = \boxed{\sqrt[\Large 3]{272}} \text{ cubic cm} = \boxed{8} \text{ centimeters}

As a final note: \boxed{8 \, \text{ cm}}

completions_8:
First, compute the volume of the original cube.
\begin{align*}
V_\text{original} & = (\text{length})^3 \quad \text{ The volume of a cube is given by the cube of its length} \\
& = (2\, \text{cm})^3 \\
& = 8\,\text{cm}^3
\end{align*}

Next, subtract the volume of the original cube from the total volume to find the volume of the new cube:
\begin{align*}
V_\text{new} - V_\text{original} & = 19\, \text{cu cm}
\end{align*}

Now, compute the volume of the new cube:
\begin{align*}
V_\text{new} & = V_\text{original} + (V_\text{new} - V_\text{original}) \\
& = 8\, \text{cu cm} + 19\, \text{cu cm} \\
& = 27\, \text{cu cm}
\end{align*}

To determine the length of one edge of the new cube, take the cube root of the volume:
\begin{align*}
\text{Length of new cube} & = \sqrt[3]{V_\text{new}} \quad \text{ The length of a cube is given by the cube root of its volume} \\
& = \sqrt[3]{27\,\text{cu cm}}
\end{align*}

The result is a cube root (not a rational number), so simplify it:
\begin{align*}
\sqrt[3]{27\,\text{cu cm}} & = 3\,\sqrt[3]{3}\,\text{cu cm} \quad\text{(The cubic root of 27 is } 3^3 = 27\text{ and the cube root of 3 is }\sqrt[3]{3} \text{.)}
\end{align*}

Now, express the length in centimeters:
\begin{align*}
\boxed{\text{Length of new cube}} & = 3\,\sqrt[3]{3}\,\text{cu cm } \text{ units }
\end{align*}

So, the length of one edge of the new cube is \boxed{ 3\,\sqrt[3]{3}\, \text{ cm }}.

